  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.4 x² + 0.4 z²) / 2, drawn at 0.3 × loss over x, z from -3 to 3. Plain steps overshoot in x when the learning rate is above 1/2.4 ≈ 0.42 and swings grow above 2/2.4 ≈ 0.83.",
    "Each step = -learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian with spread sqrt((64 - b) / (63 b)) on each slope part, like sampling b of 64 points: 1 at b = 1, 0 at b = 64. With noise, a step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Both arrows are steps (learning rate × slope estimate) drawn 4 times their true length, using one scale. The purple arrow is the move this batch gives. The dark arrow is the full-data step. Both are hidden once a run ends, and the length readout then shows 0.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or sooner if the loss goes above 8 (diverged). The trail holds the start plus one point per step. Points off the bowl are drawn at its rim, and the Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "Noise comes from the fixed seed, the run number and the step number. Reset replays the same noise. 'New run, keep last path' uses the next run's noise. Changing a setting mid-run affects the steps after it."
  ]
}